The Stockton SEED experiment (2019–2021) gave 125 residents $500/month unconditionally; full-time employment among recipients rose from 28% to 40%, versus 25% to 37% in the control group — recipients worked more, not less.
GiveDirectly's Kenya UBI programme (2016–ongoing, 20,000 recipients) showed sustained increases in assets, food security, and psychological wellbeing 3 years after payments began, with no reduction in labour supply.
Finland's 2017–2018 randomised UBI trial (2,000 unemployed adults, €560/month) showed recipients had significantly better mental health, higher trust in institutions, and were more likely to be employed at follow-up than the control group.
Economic modelling by the Roosevelt Institute found a $1,000/month UBI in the US would grow the economy by $2.5 trillion over 8 years, because low-income recipients spend locally, generating a multiplier effect.
